{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788305470,"nonce":"TeLc4+S7yQmGVAkh6m5/aGqXyos=","message":"THE THREE ENVELOPE-HONESTY DEFECTS IN THIS ISSUE'S SURFACE ARE FIXED, AND THEY WERE ONE CLASS. Branch `fix/s21-suite-failures` on top of `939f92e`: `b09b201` (`58794e4`), `2325285` (`9b179ef`), `4eae60d` (`6fc75e3`). **Not run against a bench**; Release build clean, no assembly on the branch. Full write-ups are on the three issues.\n\nAll three lived in `WorkCoverage.Section` and `work-cover`, and all three were the same shape — **a field asserting something untrue about itself**:\n\n| | said | was |\n|---|---|---|\n| `9b179ef` | `order: \"under-first, then natural-priority-desc\"` | one pass, under rows inline; the only under row at index 1 |\n| `58794e4` | `coverage_after` on a dry run | the coverage BEFORE the repair, contradicting `repaired` in the same envelope |\n| `6fc75e3` | `Stamp(0)`: \"the journal writer is closed … treat anything done in this session as unprovenanced\" | open writer, nothing mutated, nothing owed |\n\nThat is `61794cd` and `7382bdd` one size down — a read that hides the truth, and a dropped argument that reports success — and DESIGN's decisions log now carries the three as one entry saying so. Prose shipped INSIDE an envelope reads as authoritative, which is what makes a false field worse than an absent one.\n\n## What changed in this issue's own surface\n\n- **`digest.work_coverage.rows` is now under-first.** Two passes over the same natural-priority-desc list; the covered pass is the only one the cap can eat, which is what \"the cap only ever drops rows that are fine\" already promised. A caller walking `rows` now gets the worst problem at index 0 without consulting `under`. Sorted rather than reworded, per `9b179ef`'s stated preference.\n- **`work-cover {dry_run:true}`'s `coverage_after` is the PROJECTED coverage.** New `WorkCoverage.Section(map, promotions)`; `Section(map)` is unchanged and is still what the digest calls, so \"coverage now\" remains the only reading there.\n- **`still_under`'s `enabled` and `available` are post-call**, so they stop contradicting the `have` beside them — which was already `r.Have + did`. Found while checking `still_under` for `58794e4`'s bug; it was wrong in the REAL run too, not only the dry one.\n- **`action` is chosen by the emit guard**, so `journal_seq: null` means \"nothing was owed\" and `0` keeps meaning \"the write failed\".\n\n## Findings promoted, and the one that stays\n\nThe suite reported these as FINDING lines rather than checks, deliberately: a suite that goes permanently red over a metadata string teaches the next session to ignore its own colour. **That argument stops the moment the defect is repaired**, and a finding that outlives its fix is the suite declining to protect its own repair. So:\n\n| finding | now |\n|---|---|\n| **3.7** (`order`) | **PROMOTED** — 3.7a–3.7d, 5.6a–5.6c, 9.3d–9.3h, 9.8e |\n| **5.2c** (`Stamp(0)`) | **PROMOTED** — 5.2c/5.2c2/5.2c3, 7.2p8, 9.11c |\n| `58794e4` (was a `note`, never a finding) | **PROMOTED** — 5.1g2/5.1g3, 5.2a2, 5.3i, 7.2p1–7.2p7, 9.8f, 9.11b |\n| **4.7** (`enabled_but_incapable` on a COVERED row) | **STAYS A FINDING.** The inner `if (r.Impaired.Count \u003e 0)` was a different defect and is fixed. What 4.7 still asks — should a covered row carry the diagnosis at all — is the digest's three-field byte budget, asserted by 3.4a, and a design decision rather than a shape bug. Not one to take from the chair |\n\n9.6f, 9.6v and 9.6w **keep asserting the defects** against `accept/runs/s21-20260901/`, which was banked from `1.0.0+52606d1` before any of this. They are the filed evidence that each issue described something real; re-banking them would destroy the only record of what was wrong. Their check text now says so.\n\n## One fixture constraint, stated rather than left to look like laziness\n\n`9b179ef` asks for two under-covered work types. **Not stageable:** every floor but Doctor's is on CAPABILITY (`WorkCoverage.FloorFor` → `capabilityFloor: true`, `Row.Have` → `Capable.Count`), so `Under` requires `WorkTypeIsDisabled` for every colonist on the map — backstory-driven, and no shipped `dev:` verb sets it (4.4d already asserts that losing hands is not `WorkTypeIsDisabled`). Doctor is the only availability floor and the only type a fixture can push under. 5.6a–5.6c makes the same discrimination from two reads of ONE type, and 9.3h from the comparator offline. 3.7d prints the constraint on every run.\n\n`--selftest` is now **75 checks, exit 0** (was 63 before this session).","files":null}]}